														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3646997">
									

<p>Diary entries weren't half bad, but it's a common enough vehicle for exposition that it sticks out as just that - and it's really hard to get into unless they're executed particularly well.</p>
<p>They're not really bad per se, but they're too matter-of-fact to be believable (especially considering how it's even recognized in the work: &quot;I’m writing now for anyone who’ll listen. Anyone who stumbles across this record and wants to know what happened here.&quot;).</p>
<p>The story you have there? It's good. Like, everything about the dissolving situation, culminating in survivors gambling for their lives? Fuck. I'd eat that shit up - just not a fan of how this is executed.</p>
<p>This was off to a strong start, I think; it opens up with a compelling tableau. I believe this would have been more interesting with a less-is-more approach: present (more vividly) the state of things as they are discovered after the fact, rather than give a primer, and explain everything with a conveniently-thorough diary.</p>
<p>The CI stuff is what cinched my downvote. Of all the possible things to have happened involving the story you've built, it seems like the least interesting angle to follow.</p>

							</div>

								
					
